 "report": "Particle A42-spiral-e000-d030: knot (k=9)\n\nWhat it is: a particle of mass 2.4 and radius 1.88 with 9 ports on its rim. Each port is a stick-slip contact with a dead zone (mean 0.45) and a 4-dim charge; 15 of its port pairs attract each other strongly (so copies can bind port to port). Inside, 32 couplings (50% inhibitory) pass a push from port to port; it settles in 3 sweeps with slip rate 0.97 and keeps its state (memory).\nHow it assembles (32 copies, 2 worlds): 1.53 bonds per particle, largest molecule 8.0 particles, 5.5 left free.\n\nIts matter vs an ESN of the same size (readout only, both with tuned input scale); controls: no bonds, random bonds:\n  memcap   0.091 vs ESN 0.999\u00b10.000  [below]  no bonds 0.087  random bonds 0.084\n  xor      0.627 vs ESN 0.517\u00b10.016  [beat]  no bonds 0.627  random bonds 0.627\n  narma10  0.273 vs ESN 0.844\u00b10.001  [below]  no bonds 0.257  random bonds 0.259\n  latch    0.963 vs ESN 0.684\u00b10.004  [beat]  no bonds 0.962  random bonds 0.965\n  mnist14  0.596 vs ESN 0.855\u00b10.018  [below]  no bonds 0.411  random bonds 0.599\n\nGood at: xor, latch.\nIts assembly does not matter: the particles do as well unbonded, so the useful part is the single particle's own stick-slip memory.\nUse (a guess): combining recent events nonlinearly (temporal feature detection); holding a value until it is overwritten, with nothing trained but the readout: a physical memory element for a recurrent or reservoir system."
